<blockquote>
<p>The idea is that every death will trigger a new set of logs, as every trip through is slightly different. Through this process, you are going to see the effect of living multiple lifetimes in Foundation custody</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Well this already doesn't make sense, as they wouldn't have records of the past lives. Either it didn't happen at all <strong>or</strong> this is an alternative universe.</em></p>
<ul>
<li>What does containment even look like for this man? How would the Foundation do anything about this? If you mean just trying to keep him alive, that's horribly passive containment.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>What evidence would the Foundation even have of an anomaly?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>More importantly tho, why would he need to be contained? He's not really a threat to the Veil.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><em>Narratively I still think this is a bit weak, there's little <strong>stakes</strong> for the Foundation to get involved or the reader to get invested. Dude is gonna live again anyway, right?</em></p>
<p>Also&#8230; and I don't wanna ask the obvious, but what sets this apart from like Groundhog Day, and every other timeloop thing? Just that he lives his whole life?</p>

