<blockquote>
<p>What would, to you, satisfy that definition of unpredictable/novel material?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Something along the lines of a quirky or unusual motivation behind the existence of the sanitarium, which would catch the audience off-guard if they're expecting a typical horror genre setup. <a href="/scp-2662">SCP-2662</a>, for example, is a great subversion of the typical cult-drawing otherworldly terror entity in that it doesn't want people to worship it, and also feels extremely uncomfortable when people do. The dialogue and behaviors/mannerisms of the entity flesh out the character in a meaningful, believable way. You can also look at the discussion thread for that article to see what readers specifically liked about the characterization.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And what is, &quot;that's just what they do&quot;? Is it the lack of explanation into what the sanitorium is/does that is dissatisfied?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No, I would need to know more about <em>why</em> the sanitarium does what it does. Though in all honesty, the &quot;what&quot; it does also needs some work because it's pretty predictable so far as horror genre material goes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How is this a generic monster and what would you recommend to improve it? I still plan to pursue this entity even if it never finds its way into a published article. So what can be done to make it unique?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's a generic monster in that it has powers and harms people and has no personality or relatable features. So far as improvement goes, it depends on how you as the author want the audience to respond to the entity. With fear? Pity? Intrigue?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Finally, is it possible to make an SCP tale without a corresponding article?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes. We have a lot of tales based on anomalies that aren't mainlisted.</p>

